@meta
id: 10-05
title: Change what is inside the quotes
chapter: 10
type: lesson
skills: obj-quote, inner-vs-around
par: 26
minutes: 4
vscode: double-click the value, hope it took the whole string, retype
open: sentry.json

@brief
A string is a thing with edges, which makes it an object. `i"` is
everything between the two quote marks on the line; `a"` is that plus the
quotes themselves.

Nearly every time you want the first one. You are replacing the value, not
the fact that it is a string.

This service has been deployed to production and is still reporting its
errors under the staging environment, so nothing shows up in the
production Sentry project. Point it at production.

The DSN is right. Only the environment is wrong.

@keys
| `i"` | everything between the quotes |
| `ci"` | change it — the fastest edit in vim |
| `a"` | the quotes as well |

@hints
The string is an object in its own right. You do not have to select it a
character at a time.
--
`ci"` deletes what is between the quotes, leaves the quotes, and puts you
in insert mode between them.
--
Get the cursor onto the value first — `f` onto a letter inside it — then
`ci"` and type the new value.

@solution
    4j fs ci" production <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Twenty-two keystrokes, ten of which are the word `production`.

The reason this matters more than it looks: a string is exactly the kind
of thing a mouse is bad at. Double-clicking inside `"staging"` gets you the
word, which is fine here and wrong the moment the string has a dot, a
dash or a space in it. Dragging from quote to quote is a two-handed
operation and you will take the quote with you about one time in five.

`ci"` cannot take the quote with it. The object is defined by the quotes,
so they are the one thing it will never touch.

The same pair exists for every kind of quote: `ci'` for single, `` ci` ``
for a template literal. Vim does not care what the file's language is —
it just looks at the line.

@start sentry.json
{
  "name": "@thrive/orders",
  "sentry": {
    "dsn": "https://4504@ingest.sentry.io/orders",
    "environment": "staging",
    "tracesSampleRate": 0.1
  }
}

@expected sentry.json
{
  "name": "@thrive/orders",
  "sentry": {
    "dsn": "https://4504@ingest.sentry.io/orders",
    "environment": "production",
    "tracesSampleRate": 0.1
  }
}
